    {'para': '**211. Declension.** The stem ⟨nau⟩ f., ‘ship, boat’, is entirely regular, taking throughout the normal endings, as given in § 90. Thus: ⟨naus⟩, ⟨nāvam⟩, ⟨nāvā⟩, etc.; ⟨nāvau⟩, ⟨naubhyām⟩, etc.; ⟨nāvas⟩, ⟨naubhis⟩, etc.'},
    {'para': '**212.** The stems ending in long vowels (⟨ā⟩, ⟨ī⟩, ⟨ū⟩) fall into two well-marked classes: A. **root-stems — mostly monosyllabic — and their compounds,** with a comparatively small number of others inflected like them; B. **derivative feminine stems** in ⟨ā⟩ and ⟨ī⟩, with a few in ⟨ū⟩, inflected like ⟨jāyā⟩, ⟨nadī⟩ and ⟨vadhū⟩. The stems of class A take the normal endings throughout, with optional exceptions in dat., abl.-gen., and loc. sing. fem., and with ⟨n⟩ inserted before ⟨ām⟩ of the gen. pl. The simple words are as nouns with few exceptions fem.; as adjectives (rare), and in adjective compounds, they coincide in masc. and fem. forms. The declension of the simple words in ⟨ī⟩ and ⟨ū⟩ has been given (in §§ 189, 197); those in ⟨ā⟩ are rare that it is not possible to make up a whole scheme of forms in actual use.'},
    {'para': '**213.** When any root in ⟨ā⟩ or ⟨ī⟩ or ⟨ū⟩ is found as final member of a compound word, these root-finals are treated as follows: 1. Roots in ⟨ā⟩ lose that vowel before vowel-endings, except in the strong cases and in the acc. pl., which is like the nominative. Thus, ⟨viśva-pā⟩ m., f., ‘all-protecting’:'},
    {'table': {'head': ['', 'Sing.', 'Dual.', 'Plural.'], 'rows': [['N.V.', '⟨viśvapās⟩ ⟨-pās⟩', '⟨viśvapau⟩ ⟨-pau⟩', '⟨viśvapās⟩ ⟨-pās⟩'], ['A.', '⟨viśvapām⟩ ⟨-pām⟩', '⟨viśvapābhyām⟩', '„ „'], ['I.', '⟨viśvapā⟩ ⟨viśvap-ā⟩', '⟨viśvapos⟩ ⟨viśvap-os⟩', '⟨viśvapābhis⟩ etc.']]}},
    {'para': '**214.** 2. Roots in ⟨ī⟩ and ⟨ū⟩ change their final vowel, before vowel-endings, into ⟨y⟩ and ⟨v⟩, if but one consonant precede the final vowel; but if two or more consonants precede, the change is into ⟨iy⟩ and ⟨uv⟩. Thus, ⟨yava-krī⟩ m., f., ‘corn-buying’: nom.-voc. ⟨yavakrīs⟩, acc. ⟨yavakriyam⟩; ⟨khala-pū⟩ m., f., ‘street-sweeper’: nom. sing. ⟨khalapūs⟩, acc. ⟨khalapvam⟩.'},
